    "turn_text": "Trump and trade got him elected, but wait a minute, why is his commerce secretary an outsorcher of job story coming up? Meanwhile, more fireworks at those cabinet confirmation hearings and today on the broadcast, Our featured guests on Sheet Metal Workers, local 33 Joe Thayer. Good afternoon and welcome to the Wednesday, January 18th edition of America's Workforce. We call it labor radio and we're broadcast from Ohio for workers around the entire world and archived on AWF Radio.com. I just want to mention to us at this yesterday on the show before we billboard the show. Tomorrow is a trades day at the Dari Home Improvement Show and I'm going to be working that show from tomorrow all the way to Sunday. And if you remember the building trades, you definitely, all you have to do is show your union card you get in for free. That's tomorrow only, so that'll save you the $14 admission. And if you want to take a buddy, That person is free too. It's for you and a guest. So do check that out at the home improvement show. You remember the trades? Bring that union card. You walk in free and you find out all kind of cool things that are happening there. Joe Thayer will be our guest on the show today. He's with the sheet metal workers, Local 33 in actually in parma located in a corporate circle there by 130th on the US side of Cleveland. What we're going to do is run down the opportunities that a lot of folks have in the trades, specifically today, sheet metal workers, and talk about this on the show so many times. You see these kids that go to college, they incur $100,000 in debt. You know how expensive college is today. And then they end up with a job, maybe in a fast food restaurant. You know what? You can avoid that. And if you have a kid, a grand kid, you might want to pay very close attention to what Joe has to say. In fact, there's a couple of dates coming up. February 13th, which is right around the corner, March 13th, April 10th, May 8th. Those are days that they'll be accepting applications. It's on the second Monday of each and every month. You got to be 17 high school diploma, GED, and need proof of that. There's a fee that he's going to run down. Now, what do you do as a sheet metalworker? Joe's going to run that down. They work with steel, non-ferrous. We're talking about aluminum, copper, alternative materials, plastics, fiberglass. They fabricate, install, maintain, and service components. in heating, ventilating, and air conditioning systems, HVAC systems. This includes air handling equipment, duct work, duct system components, the drawings for shop fabrication and field installation. They also perform the testing, adjusting, and balancing of the complete HVAC system. We're talking including dust and fume collection systems, machine guards, spray booths, ovens, They also fabricate install and maintain components used in architectural applications, metal roofing, siding, decking, coping, flashing, decorative materials. There's a lot, and this is done both indoors and outdoors in varying environmental conditions. In the shop, the components are actually laid out on flat sheets formed and then assembled. And in the field, the components are installed per the job plans and the specifications. The trade requires the physical ability for lifting, carrying, and installing all of those components. So you're talking about ladder scaffolds, powered man lifts. Various heights you got to work with can't be afraid of heights if you're a sheet metal worker, but we're going to get into all of this. Run down their program as I indicated, it's a five year program, minimum of 8,000 hours on the job training, minimum of 144 hours of related technical instruction. It's almost like a college course when you think about it. And here's the beauty. You get paid while you're learning this and you get a really nice paying job with good benefits when you're done. So Joe is going to run all of that down as our guest here on America's workforce. What we're going to do later in the broadcast These confirmation hearings for Trump's cabinet picks have been off the charts here. I'm going to talk about Wilbur Ross here in a minute, but what we're going to do, and this is courtesy of the Labor Federation and their blogs that they do on AFLCOIO.org. What we did is pulled a segment from last week's hearing on the the labor secretary Andrew Puzder. He's the guy that runs Hardee's and Carl's junior two restaurants that have been accused of wage and our violations and this is Trump's cabinet pick for labor So it's like the Fox Garden the chicken coop. He's a multi multi millionaire He makes about $17,000 per day which is more than the people making his restaurants in one year. Yeah, we got some stats on this. The wage and hour division of the Labor Department cited approximately 60% of Hardee's and Carl Jr. restaurants for shorting workers on pay since Puzder took over in 2000. So he's held that job for almost 17 years. It interviews with the Restaurant Opportunity Center. individual workers filled in those blanks as part of the extensive survey of workers i've experienced wage theft said one former hearties worker our general manager would occasionally have a handle laundry duties then proceed not to pay me for the work that i did i was promised i would be paid in cash but to this day I haven't seen a single penny. Now some of these folks testified at the hearings last week and we're going to play that. And Liz Warren, along with Al Franken, both of them have been doing a bang up job yesterday. They were grilling Betsy DeVos, who is Trump's pick for the education department. who has no background in public education. All she has done is be a big proponent of charter schools. And she's twisted arms and legs because she's a multi-multi-billionaire. Now what I'm going to play for you is a little snippet on the Commerce Secretary. And Wilbur Ross is going before the committee today. Wilbur Ross, you may recall that name. Especially if you worked at LTV steel LTV steel file bankruptcy more than 20 years ago Wilbur Ross was the guy that came in there Worked a new deal. I know a lot of the workers were happy that he came in because they still had a job, but what he did pretty much Reformed everything in the steel industry and he's done that in the coal mine industry as well Let's listen to this report of his background because here's a guy as head of commerce and who's supposed to try to, you know, preserve and bring more jobs in the United States. Instead, he's done just the opposite. Let's listen here on America's workforce.",
