  {
    "episode_id": 71067,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71067.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "Well, the program that we have is a five-year apprenticeship program for sheet metal work, which when you think about sheet metal work, a lot of the folks that come in and ask about it, they I guess the stereotype is they believe it's like factory work, maybe making parts for the automotive industry or maybe custom fabrication. But the reality is it encompasses a lot of different things. And what we train over the five years is teaching the apprentices, guys and girls both how to draft rub blueprints, read blueprints, CAD, which is the computer automated drafting. teach them how to actually make duct work for HVAC systems, residential and commercial, teach them how to install it. So HVAC heating in their conditioning is a big component of sheet metal work, but it's not the only part of it. We get into refrigeration, welding, industrial type work. We have guys that are always working in the automotive plants, steel mills, foundries. We have companies that architectural sheet metal work, they fabricate and install",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
