  {
    "episode_id": 71067,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71067.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "working for the contractor, so you're 40 hour, maybe 40 hour plus weeks, depending if there's overtime. In the field training, working with the companies and the journeymen out in the field, but there's also classroom time. So you'll spend, like I said, a couple of months out in the field working for whomever. And then the classroom time is split up throughout that year. Typically how it goes is there's day school and night school. So you'll have the first four years in the apprenticeship. There's four weeks of day school that are split up throughout the year. So you'll come to school those weeks for a full 40-hour week instead of going to work. And the beauty of that is, while you're in school, you're actually gonna get paid while you're in school so you don't miss a check. You get 50% of your wages while you're in school plus your benefits, which was negotiated on behalf of the guys. So then you go back to work that following week and really don't miss a beat. Then there's also some night school and some Saturdays here and there, but ultimately the commitment you're going to make, the payoff in the end is the money and benefits that you're gonna make when you graduate the program.",
