  {
    "episode_id": 71067,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71067.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 106,
    "turn_text": "Good afternoon. Welcome back to America's workforce broadcast from Ohio for workers around the entire world and archived on awf radio.com. Instead of having a live guest, what we're going to do right now is take you to last week's Senate hearings, confirmation hearings for Andrew Puzder, former Clevelander grew up in Northeastern Ohio and he is Trump's nominee to head the Labor Department. And a lot of people do not think that he is qualified for the position among them. Elizabeth Warren, let's listen here on America's workforce.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_09",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "This is a broadcast titled 'America's Workforce' originating from Ohio.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The broadcast is available worldwide to workers and also archived online at awf radio.com.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Andrew Puzder is the nominee to lead the U.S. Department of Labor.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Today's segment will feature recorded footage from last week's Senate confirmation hearings.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Andrew Puzder was nominated by Donald Trump.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Andrew Puzder is originally from Northeastern Ohio and previously lived in Cleveland.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "There is public skepticism regarding Andrew Puzder's qualifications for the role.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Viewers expect informative and credible reporting on political appointments.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Audience members value access to authoritative sources like official Senate hearings.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Public criticism of nominees adds credibility to the broadcast's coverage.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Listeners trust the broadcaster’s selection of relevant political figures and topics.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "The term 'former Clevelander' implies familiarity with local culture and background as relevant context.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 71067,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71067.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 107,
    "turn_text": "President-elect Trump has selected nominees to run his government, and if confirmed by the United States Senate, they will have enormous power. Their decisions will touch the lives of every single person in this country. Their decisions will affect the safety of the food we eat, the cleanliness of the air we breathe, and the water we drink. They will decide if millions of Americans continue to have medical care or if they will rip it away. Whether to stand up for students that are being crushed by student loans or to stand aside while their futures are stolen away. Whether to ring in Wall Street or once again let them run wild. Whether to stand up for working Americans or to let big companies just roll right over them. And that is why our Constitution requires that the Senate provide its advice and consent before any senior government nominee can take on a job. It is one of the most important responsibilities we have, and every senator should take it extremely seriously. It is especially important today, because while the president-elect ran for office on the promise to drain the swamp in Washington and to stand up for working people, many of his cabinet nominees are billionaires and Washington influence peddlers who have long histories of doing exactly the opposite. Despite the Constitution's clear requirements, it is clear that the Republicans who run the Senate have no intention of seriously examining the President's nominees. The Senate Republicans are handing out free passes to all of the Trump nominees no matter what. Most cabinet nominees have not completed their required FBI background checks or ethics requirements to ensure that they will put first the national interest and not their own personal business interests. Last Friday, the head of the non-partisan office of government ethics said he had great concern that Republicans were rushing hearings on these nominees and that his office is quote, under pressure to cut corners and ignore conflicts of interest, ignore conflicts of interest. Now, those are serious allegations failing to comply with these ethics requirements is playing with fire. Violating ethics rules could result in jail time for cabinet nominees. But Mitch McConnell's response was to call them little procedural complaints. The Trump transition team may not mind running a rubber stamp confirmation process. And Senator McConnell may not care if these nominees follow our criminal and ethics laws or are even qualified to serve. But many of us in the Senate, Democrats and Republicans take our constitutional responsibilities seriously and we will work hard to fulfill those obligations in the coming days and weeks ahead. Today's forum is part of our effort to meet those responsibilities. On December 8th, Donald Trump nominated Andrew Puzder to serve as the Secretary of Labor. Now, if you work for a living, this man is important to you. The Secretary of Labor is responsible for protecting the interests of 150 million American workers. He will be the person responsible for enforcing the laws that ensure that employers actually pay workers for every hour that they work and setting standards to prevent workplace injuries and death. Unfortunately, Mr. Puster is not the kind of American person that workers can trust to stand up for them. Since 2000, Mr. Puster has served as the CEO of the Billion Dollar Company, CKE restaurant holdings. Now, many of you may know it better as the parent company of Carl's Jr. and Hardys. These two fast food chains are known for paying low wages to their workers. Mr. Puster also has a long record of cheating workers out of overtime. And he has paid out millions of dollars to settle claims when he was caught cheating. According to a survey of 550 Carl's Juniors and Hardy's employees just released this morning by the Restaurant Opportunities Coalition United, about a third of respondents reported that they were illegally forced to work extra hours without pay. Over a third of the respondents reported unpaid overtime or not receiving the breaks that the law requires. 79% reported working sick either because they couldn't get the day off or they needed the money and over half experienced unwanted sexual advances at work. These are not isolated incidences. They reflect the kind of business that Mr. Puster has built. Mr. Puster is a frequent political pundit and commentator who has vocally opposed higher minimum wages. He has also strongly opposed new overtime protections that would give 4 million Americans a $1.5 billion raise in a single year. Mr. Puster also delights in expressing his personal disdain for his workers. He bragged in his very first memo as CEO that he wrote that he wanted, quote, No more people behind the counter unless they have their teeth. Ha, ha. He also said he would like to replace his workers with robots because, quote, they're always polite. They always upsell. They never take a vacation. There's never a slip and fall or an age, sex or discrimination case. Mr. Puster will have an opportunity to explain these views and his actions at his upcoming confirmation hearing. But let's be clear, talk is cheap. The Senate has an obligation to hear from those who are best qualified to tell America about Mr. Puzder's suitability to be the Secretary of Labor and to stand up for American workers, his own workers. And that is why my colleagues and I asked the chairman of the Help Committee last week to include Mr. Puzder's workers in his confirmation hearing. When he refused to do so, we had no choice but to hold today's forum ourselves. So I would like to introduce our panelists. Laura McDonald worked as a general manager at Carl's Jr. in Tucson, Arizona for 20 years. For years, she was forced to work extra hours without pay. Employees like Laura are the subject of a major lawsuit against Mr. Puzder's company regarding unpaid overtime. Lupe Guzman is a single mother who has devoted the last seven years of her life to Carl's Jr. in Las Vegas, Nevada, working the graveyard shift for rock bottom wages. She'll tell us what it's like for a devoted Carl's Jr. employee struggling to build a future for herself and her family. Roberto Ramirez has worked in the fast food industry for over 20 years now, mostly at Carl's Jr. in Los Angeles. He regularly worked off the clock at Carl's Jr. and even had a full paycheck stolen by his manager. We will also hear testimony from the Executive Director of the National Employment Law Project, Chris Owens. And she will explain how the stories of our witnesses are consistent with the experience of scores of CKE witnesses who are afraid to speak out publicly. So I want to thank all of you for being here today. It isn't easy to speak out against your employer Especially when you have to come to the United States Senate to do it. But it is vital that your voices are heard as part of this process. Your country owes you a debt of gratitude for your willingness to speak today. Thank you. Now, we're going to keep moving things around to try their multiple hearings going on. And I'm going to ask, actually, I shot past this. Would you also like to introduce one of our witnesses, the one from Nevada? I was looking right and didn't see that you were there.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
