  {
    "episode_id": 71067,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71067.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 110,
    "turn_text": "Thank you so much, Senator. And let me also echo. Thank you all for being here. It is not an easy thing. You've got a room crowded with people. But you've come here to tell your story, which is an important story. And I want people to understand Listen, when we are talking about people that are struggling and just want economic security and are fighting for their families in every opportunities, they're all our faces. They all look like all of us. And we should be fighting to ensure that everybody has an opportunity to succeed. My name is Catherine Cortez Masto. I'm the Senator from Nevada. And I have the opportunity to introduce, and it is my pleasure. to introduce a fellow Nevada and Lupe Guzman. Thank you so much for coming, Lupe. Lupe, if you don't know, you've heard a little bit about her introduction. She works the graveyard shift at Carl's Jr. in Las Vegas. And she is a single mom with six beautiful kids, as I can see right there, that she brought with them in the photograph. She's been working hard at Carl's Jr. for the past seven years and playing by the rules and fighting to make ends meet for her family. Yet despite years of hard work and her senior role, Lupe only makes $8.75 an hour, just barely above the minimum wage. And she relies on programs like SNAP, Medicaid, and Housing Assistance to provide for her family. You know, we've made a lot of progress over the last eight years, but that progress too often has benefited the wealthy instead of lifting up families like Lupe's. In today's economy, it can seem almost impossible for families like hers to even keep up. It's a stark reminder that the minimum wage is a poverty wage. If it weren't for Republican obstruction, it would have been raised a long time ago. And unfortunately, we have an incoming president who thinks that wages are too high in this country. We need an economy that works for everyone. But how do we achieve that when employers like Carl's Jr. are paving paying poverty wages, shorting paychecks, failing to pay overtime, declining to offer health insurance, and not even doing enough to keep their workers safe on the job. It takes a lot of courage for Lupe and all of you to be here today and speak up. And we so appreciate you coming to Washington today to do just that. I look forward to hearing all of your stories And I will just say we need to continue to keep up the fight. And all of your stories are a reminder of how much work we have to do to help hard-working families across the country and why many of us are so concerned about President-elect's nominee to be the next Labor Secretary. Thank you, Lupe, for being here today and representing Las Vegas and Nevada.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
